|~ What is declared under `::null`, the kind of the absent value: the
   soft read of it. ~|

:null/PayloadNotNullError
  |~~ `::null` reads its payload as null; a payload of another kind is
      refused.
      ~(
        ::null(1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::null/PayloadNotNullError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:null/at
  |~~ The default the call gives, null when it gives none, whatever the
      place: a soft read of the absent value is absent, so soft reads
      chain, `at :address | at :city`, and a `coalesce` after them gives
      the fallback once. The strict `/k` refuses null as it refuses
      every value without parts.

      ~(null | at :k | eq null)
      ~(null | at :k "none" | eq "none")
      ~({:a null} | at :a | at :b | coalesce "none" | eq "none")
      ~({:a {:b 1}} | at :a | at :b | eq 1)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:null/at/place
  |~~ The place the call reads. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:null/at/default |~~ The value answered for a place the subject does not hold, null when it gives none. ~~| ~(at 1)

